  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 205 AM Thursday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Heat Advisories likely needed for Friday. At least \na low chance of PM thunderstorms Friday. \n\nWithout satellite data, it is a bit challenging tonight. Strong \nridging today with nearly full sun and afternoon mixing of the dew \npoints down into the lower to mid 60s will keep heat indices a bit \nbelow the 105 degree criteria for two consecutive hours. Obviously, \nit will be hot as highs reach the mid to upper 90s. These readings \nwill be well above the mid-July normals by 5-8 degrees. Lows tonight \nwill be hard pressed to fall into the mid to upper 70s, likely \nremaining well into the 80s in urban areas through midnight.\n\nFriday, there is support from all sources that highs will be mainly \nin the 96-101 range, with heat indices reaching 105+ for a few hours \nin the afternoon in the eastern Piedmont, Coastal Plain, and \nSandhills. The NW Piedmont will be close, but around 100-103. So, \nadvisories will likely be needed for heat Friday.\n\nIn addition, a few strong thunderstorms will be possible aided by \nMLCapes 2000+ j/kg, increasing low level moisture, high heat, and a \nsurface lee trough.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Increasingly unsettled weather expected Saturday \ninto the weekend, with above normal rainfall possible across eastern \nNC.\n\n\ufeffThe upper ridging responsible for the late week hot spell will \nbreak down by Saturday as broad upper troughing is established over \nthe Great Lakes and Northeast. After a shortwave moves through the \nupper trough on Saturday, a cold front is forecast to eventually \nsink south into the Mid-Atlantic States and perhaps south into VA/NC \nby Sunday/Monday. Meanwhile, an upper wave currently over the \nwestern Caribbean is forecast to drift to the north, resulting in a \ntrough or area of low pressure in the northeast Gulf and generally \nlower heights aloft all along the Appalachians and Southeast. This \nwill lead to increasingly unsettled weather, moreso Sunday into \nearly next week, with the potential for the low over the northeast \nGulf to briefly obtain some tropical structure and drift to the \nSoutheast coast. Regardless of the specific evolution of a low, \nincreased moisture, particularly if tapped from the Atlantic, along \nwith the potential for anomalous PW and with a stalled boundary over \nthe region will be favorable near or above normal rainfall over at \nleast eastern NC. Current official QPF paints one inch or greater \ntotals east of US 1, although longer range ensembles  suggest those \none inch or greater totals could extend further west by mid to late \nweek.",
